WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.340
British explorer Bear Grylls is best known
to TV audiences for Discovery's Man vs. Wild

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.800
— known as Born Survivor: Bear Grylls in
the UK.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:11.980
The magic of TV makes Grylls look like the
ultimate survivor, but the truth isn't quite

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:12.860
so clear-cut.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:17.620
From his genuine scrapes with death to embarrassing
leaks about his so-called survival shows,

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.200
this is the untold truth of Bear Grylls.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:21.700
School bullies

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.980
Anyone who's seen his shows might be surprised
to learn that Grylls was bullied in school.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.080
The guy seems pretty tough now, but he wasn't
always like that.

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:32.940
In response to the bullying he received at
school, young Grylls decided to take up karate

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.360
along with a few of his friends.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.240
Three years later he got his black belt and
went to train in Japan as the youngest member

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.220
of the Karate Union of Great Britain.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:43.640
Grylls described the experience in his book
Mud, Sweat and Tears:

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:47.920
"Each night we slept on the floor in small
wooden Japanese huts, and by day we learned

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.660
how to fight — real and hard.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:53.760
The training was more exacting and demanding
than anything I had previously encountered."

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:55.300
Military man

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:59.300
After leaving school, Grylls spent several
months hiking the Indian Himalayas.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:00.740
He told The Hindustan Times:

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:03.460
"I spent quite some time in India before I
joined the army.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:06.860
I went out there climbing, and up in West
Bengal and all around Darjeeling.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.580
We were in Calcutta for a while and then we
were with the Indian Army as well."

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.160
Grylls even flirted with the idea of joining
the Indian Army himself at the time, but ultimately

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:17.940
decided to enroll in the military at home
in Great Britain.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:23.440
Grylls joined the United Kingdom Special Forces
Reserve in 1994 and served with 21 Regiment

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:28.460
Special Air Service, or SAS, for three years,
receiving training in everything from desert

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:32.140
and winter warfare to evasive driving, climbing,
and explosives.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:33.560
​Freefall

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.460
Grylls might have only been an SAS reserve,
but that doesn't mean he got to skip out on

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:38.780
any of the dangerous stuff.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:42.560
Grylls' second deployment to Africa ended
in a terrible accident that almost claimed

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:43.140
his life.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:44.520
He told The Guardian:

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.490
"In Africa, my parachute ripped at 17,000
[feet].

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:49.840
I blacked out, and on landing broke my back.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.470
I spent the next 18 months in braces and plaster.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.860
I was lucky to survive, let alone walk again."

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:57.640
A friend of Grylls who was with him at the
time told The Guardian:

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:01.460
"I look back and think of him in the body
brace after that horrific parachute fall,

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:03.160
and it's incredible that he survived it.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:06.960
Then to look at what he has achieved since
then, I'd never have thought it possible."

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:08.360
Top of the world

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.180
Breaking three vertebrae after a 17,000 foot
fall would be enough to put most people off

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:15.560
heights for good, but Grylls didn't let it
stop him.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:19.640
Grylls lived his childhood dream to climb
Everest when he scaled earth's highest mountain

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.520
in 1998, less than two years after his near-fatal
Africa fall.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.640
Everest is obviously no walk in the park,
and Grylls almost met his end for a second

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:30.540
time when some loose ice left him hanging
on for dear life.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:34.020
"We were in the first stage of the Everest
ascent when the ground gave way, leaving me

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:37.600
swinging on the end of this rope, clutching
at these black and glassy walls."

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:38.980
Dinner party

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.360
In 2005, Grylls and fellow explorer Lieutenant
Commander Alan Veal staged one of the weirdest

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:45.620
stunts of Grylls' career.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:49.620
They broke the record for the world's highest
dinner party when they flew a hot air balloon

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:55.849
to a height of 24,262 feet and climbed down
to a dinner table suspended below the balloon,

00:02:55.849 --> 00:02:58.400
braving temperatures of minus 50 degrees Celsius.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:02.760
After enjoying a three-course meal together,
the daredevil duo dedicated the venture to

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:06.960
Her Royal Majesty Queen Elizabeth and skydived
back down to ground with full bellies.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:08.840
Controversies

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:14.120
Grylls was the subject of controversy in 2008
when U.S. survival consultant Mark Wienert

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:18.720
started talking to the British media about
his time working on Grylls' show Man vs. Wild,

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.300
claiming that the star was a fraud.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:24.780
Wienert revealed that during one episode he
personally assembled a Polynesian-style bamboo

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:28.779
raft off-camera only for Grylls to add the
finishing touches and take the credit.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:33.100
And after shooting on the episode concluded,
Wienert claims the ex-military man left for

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:33.600
a motel.

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:37.420
And that wasn't the only time the show allegedly
faked Grylls' whereabouts, according to the

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:37.920
whistleblower.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.700
Grylls apparently spent a few nights in a
luxurious lodge during the filming of the

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:43.360
Sierra Nevada mountains episode.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:47.680
Grylls responded to the claims by saying,
"If people felt misled on how the first series

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.820
was represented, I'm really sorry for that...we
film these things over six days and, after

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:54.800
filming the night stuff, we're back with a
crew in a base camp lodge."

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:56.280
Adventure Dad

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:59.140
When your dad eats bugs and gets peed on for

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:01.160
a living, you're bound to have an interesting
childhood.

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:04.120
That's definitely true for Bear Grylls' kids.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:07.040
When they aren't staying on the houseboat
they have moored on the banks of London's

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:11.160
River Thames, Grylls and his family live on
a remote island off the coast of Wales.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:15.840
In 2013, Grylls found himself in hot water
with the local council after erecting a huge

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:19.840
metal slide that ended with a drop off a cliff
face into the sea below.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:21.940
Grylls defended his decision by saying:

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:26.020
"The slide is not for the paying public and
therefore the health and safety is not for

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:26.820
other people.

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:29.500
It's for me and the kids and friends to use
when we are there.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:33.020
It has an element of danger to it, you do
hit the water pretty hard.

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:34.280
But do you know what?

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:36.300
There are a lot more dangerous things around."

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:41.360
He came under criticism again in 2015 when
he purposely stranded his 11-year-old son

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:45.920
on a rocky outcrop off the island to serve
as a training aid for the Royal National Lifeboat

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:46.560
Institution.

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:48.500
Survivor Games

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:53.220
Bear Grylls is hugely popular in the U.S.
and his native U.K., but he also has a massive

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:56.060
following in an unlikely place: China.

00:04:56.290 --> 00:05:01.000
Grylls' 2012 bestseller Mud, Sweat and Tears
was voted the most influential book in China

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.060
the year it was released.

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:07.120
And in 2016, Grylls invited several Chinese
celebrities to join him in the wild, including

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:12.410
former NBA star Yao Ming, Olympic swimmer
Fu Yuanhui, and Robin Li, one of China's richest

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:13.140
tech moguls.

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:17.760
The show aired under the name Survivor Games
and featured a premise similar to Mission

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:21.690
Survive, a UK series in which Grylls takes
eight celebrities on a two week hike through

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:24.300
unforgiving terrain and eliminates them one
by one.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:28.440
Despite the country's love for Grylls, however,
the Chinese version didn't go over well.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:32.590
Viewers complained that the show was "tasteless"
and "disgusting" after Grylls forced the contestants

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:33.680
to drink their own urine.

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:34.640
Disgusting?

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:35.460
Oh yeah.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:36.660
But tasteless?

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:38.120
We'll have to trust Grylls on that.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:40.480
"God there's no getting away from it."

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:40.980
"Pftttt!"

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:44.020
"That really is pretty horrible it's like
warm.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:45.880
And it's salty."

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:46.880
Thanks for watching!

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:49.250
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:51.600
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

